1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,739
Same room. Same moment.

2
00:00:02,189 --> 00:00:03,493
Two different menus.

3
00:00:03,943 --> 00:00:11,770
The doctor can read something the engineer cannot, and neither of them can open the captain's log alone.

4
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:19,611
That is the whole trick of a boarding party, and it is one word and one semicolon.

5
00:00:20,811 --> 00:00:29,507
A choice you already know, with two things added. if medical: only someone whose job is medical is offered it.

6
00:00:29,957 --> 00:00:34,304
That word is the one on Roles in your roster.

7
00:00:34,754 --> 00:00:43,885
Then a semicolon, and learn suits: when she takes it, the party knows a fact, and I have named it suits.

8
00:00:44,335 --> 00:00:52,596
And the choice needs somewhere to go: a short room that says what she found, with one way back.

9
00:00:53,796 --> 00:00:56,404
The same again for the engineer.

10
00:00:56,854 --> 00:01:02,507
Now each of my two people has one thing only they can read.

11
00:01:03,707 --> 00:01:08,489
learned is a number: how many different facts the party has.

12
00:01:08,939 --> 00:01:11,113
The party, not the person.

13
00:01:11,563 --> 00:01:18,954
So this choice appears for everyone once the surgeon and the engineer have each done their part.

14
00:01:19,404 --> 00:01:24,187
And a fact counts once, however many times they read it.

15
00:01:25,387 --> 00:01:29,735
A party that walks here first finds a locked log.

16
00:01:30,185 --> 00:01:34,533
If I say nothing, they think the game is broken.

17
00:01:34,983 --> 00:01:41,070
So two versions of the line, and a condition in curly brackets on each.

18
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:45,867
Too early, they are told there is more to find.

19
00:01:46,317 --> 00:01:49,361
Later, they are told it is time.

20
00:01:50,561 --> 00:01:51,561
Three rules.

21
00:01:52,011 --> 00:01:58,098
Nobody gets an empty menu, so every room keeps a choice with no if.

22
00:01:58,548 --> 00:02:01,591
A reading goes somewhere and comes back.

23
00:02:02,041 --> 00:02:08,128
And count your facts: two facts, and the log asks for two. Lint. Clean.

24
00:02:08,578 --> 00:02:10,752
It catches a misspelled learn.

25
00:02:11,202 --> 00:02:14,680
It catches the sign the wrong way round.

26
00:02:15,130 --> 00:02:18,174
It does not catch a misspelled job.

27
00:02:18,624 --> 00:02:26,450
That one you check by eye: every word after if is learned, or a job from your roster.

28
00:02:27,650 --> 00:02:28,650
Two people.

29
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:32,578
The doctor has it; the chief does not.

30
00:02:33,028 --> 00:02:39,550
Straight to the bridge: locked, and it says so. Back. She reads. Aft. He reads.

31
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,174
Forward: and there it is.

32
00:02:42,624 --> 00:02:46,537
Now watch what happens when the doctor stays home.

33
00:02:46,987 --> 00:02:53,074
Same room, one person, and her reading comes to me, marked: covering for medical.

34
00:02:53,524 --> 00:02:56,567
A short crew still finishes the story.

35
00:02:57,767 --> 00:03:02,115
Your third crew member has a job you made up.

36
00:03:02,565 --> 00:03:05,609
Give them a reading on the bridge.

37
00:03:06,059 --> 00:03:14,320
Leave the log at two, and now any two of three will open it: more than one way through.

38
00:03:14,770 --> 00:03:22,161
Next time, we ask not only what someone's job is, but how good they are at it.
